all right I hope everybody had a good  weekend because it's it's getting going  it's almost time for the first 2020  Democratic primary debates this thing is  really getting going in earnest I guess  we could say and there are a lot of  different things I want to talk about  related to this first series debate one  is really two debates okay there are 20  candidates who are participating in the  first debate which will be on NBC News  at the end of next week  will I be live-streaming commentary of  the debates  maybe we'll see we'll see a depends on  if there's interest that's a lot of has  a lot of live streaming in addition to  the regular show but there's a good  chance I will do it so the reason  they're splitting this up into two parts  even though it's debate number one is  that it's not practical to have 20  people debate all at once on one stage  so they're splitting it into debate 1a  which will be next Wednesday the 26 and  then debate 1b which will be Thursday  the 27th ten candidates on each part so  the obvious question who gets to be in  part one who gets to be in part two and  one thing that the DNC and NBC News I  guess together wanted to avoid is the  idea of a sort of top tier debate versus  the kids table so to speak and really  mix up people that are polling well and  people that are polling less well and  the way that NBC News says they did it  is by separating the candidates into two  groups the met the the top polling  people and the lesser well polling  people and including some from both  groups in both debates to make sure that  there is some mix now the requirements  to even qualify for this debate were  either one percent in three qualified  polls or 65 thousand donors with certain  minimums per state and a certain minimum  number of states the particulars don't  so much matter the important thing is  that lots of people who would not have  made it on the basis of polling did  receive the sufficient number of  donations and on that basis are going to  be participating in the debates which i  think is good if you're able to show I  mean I hate that money plays the role  that it plays in our elections but just  because you're not  1% if you otherwise are generating a  significant number of donations I do  think you should be allowed to  participate I think a lot of these  people polling under 1 and under two are  going bye-bye before the next series of  debates but that's the whole point of  doing these things many of these people  will be gone from the next set of  debates unless they really impress the  audience in these first debates and  that's a great thing so let's look at  the list what you will notice is that on  Wednesday June 26th  you have Cory Booker Elizabeth Warren  battle Roarke Amy Klobuchar John Delaney  Tulsi Gabbard who Leon Castro Tim Ryan  Bill DeBlasio and Washington governor  jay Inslee effectively what has happened  is that the first debate is basically  Elizabeth Warren versus a bunch of  people polling almost nothing plus Cory  Booker and beto who are polling a couple  of points but don't seem to have any  reasonable shot in my mind at impressing  people so sincerely and so  overwhelmingly that they are going to be  in this thing we'll talk in a second  whether that would be good for Elizabeth  Warren or not and then in the second  night of these debates you have the  heavier hitter frontrunner you've got  Bernie Sanders and Kamala Harris of  course Vice President Joe Biden and Pete  Budaj edge who are the frontrunners with  the exception of Elizabeth Warren who  will be on the first night and then  joining them michael bennett marianne  williamson who i have a hard time  believing she even really has the the  financial support required but ok I  don't I'm not being conspiratorial I'm  just shocked that she got 65,000  donations Eric's wall well Kirsten  Gillibrand Andrew yang has made the  stage which will be interesting John  Hickenlooper as well so the second night  is every other frontrunner but Elizabeth  Warren meaning Biden Sanders Budaj edge  and Kamala Harris does this help her  hurt Elizabeth Warren there's two ways  to think about it okay one approach is  this is bad for Elizabeth Warren because  the second night will have a bigger  audience because there are more big  players who want to see their favorite  debate rather than people they aren't  supporting the second night has more  polling support in the aggregate in  total  and so Elizabeth Warren just is not  going to get the exposure that some of  these other candidates are going to get  there's another view though which is now  this is good for Elizabeth Warren first  of all this is the first debate of the  season meaning it's going to be very  well viewed it's gonna have a  significant audience and therefore it's  not right to assume that night two will  have more audience night one is going to  have more audience the other argument to  be made in favor of Elizabeth Warren is  she is clearly the top dog on the first  night therefore she's going to do really  well against lesser known less popular  candidates the the sort of wrinkle in  that argument is that although Elizabeth  Warren is clearly the front-runner of  the first night of candidates she's not  necessarily the best debater of that  first night because as I've spoken about  before I love Elizabeth Warren's  policy-based approach it's been helping  her in the polls will talk about those  polling numbers momentarily which  continue to improve for Elizabeth Warren  but in large stage debates I don't  actually think she's that great so it'll  be very interesting to see whether  ultimately this is good or bad for  Elizabeth Warren to be the segregated  frontrunner in night one net net net I  think it probably hurts her a little bit  I think it will give her a great  opportunity to pull some support from  some of the other people she's debating  against but that in the context of  against the bigger frontrunner like  Biden and Sanders it's a missed  opportunity for her by being on a  different night but this is just the  bait one a right there's going to be a  lot of opportunities so this is this is  just the beginning what I also am really  interested in seeing is the Bernie  versus Biden dynamic in night two of the  debate which again this is next week not  this week we've just learned what the  lineups are going to be Bernie during  that Fox News Town Hall said Oh Joe's a  friend of mine but we have policy  differences or whatever however it was  that he worded it that's going to be  very interesting and it's also going to  be interesting to see if any of what I  call the one-percenters explode because  there have been listen Andrew yang who I  like has a following that says as soon  as Andrew yang gets on a debate stage  with these other candidates he is going  to explode  in the polling because people are just  going to absolutely love what he has to  say we'll see if that happens I'm  excited to see if that happens Tulsi has  a similar following that says as soon as  Tulsi Gabbard is given the opportunity  to debate these other candidates she  will come through as the right path  forward for the United States of America  well we're going to see very soon she's  polling I think 0.4% right now or maybe  it's up to like 0.7 we'll see um jay  inslee is a guy who has almost no  support but his supporters believe that  he is really offering the best package  of policies and that as soon as America  is fully introduced to jay Inslee jay  Inslee 'he's polling is going to take  off like a rocket ship or whatever  metaphor you want to use we'll see and  I'm excited to see it and we'll talk  more about the pre-debate stuff that's  going on later in the week but probably  more early early next week

